☕ Java: Compiler AND Interpreter? Both!
Most languages are one or the other. Java is BOTH — and that's its superpower.

𝗛𝗲𝗿𝗲'𝘀 𝗵𝗼𝘄 𝗶𝘁 𝘄𝗼𝗿𝗸𝘀:

𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟭 — 𝗬𝗼𝘂 𝘄𝗿𝗶𝘁𝗲 .𝗷𝗮𝘃𝗮 𝗰𝗼𝗱𝗲
Your human-readable code gets compiled by the Java Compiler (javac) into something called Bytecode (.class files). This isn't machine code yet — it's a middle layer.

𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟮 — 𝗝𝗩𝗠 𝘁𝗮𝗸𝗲𝘀 𝗼𝘃𝗲𝗿
The Java Virtual Machine (JVM) reads that bytecode and interprets it line by line. This is why Java is "Write Once, Run Anywhere" — every OS has its own JVM, so the same bytecode runs everywhere.

𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟯 — 𝗝𝗜𝗧 𝗖𝗼𝗺𝗽𝗶𝗹𝗲𝗿 𝗸𝗶𝗰𝗸𝘀 𝗶𝗻 🔥
The Just-In-Time (JIT) Compiler watches your code at runtime and identifies frequently executed ("hot") code paths. It compiles those parts directly into native machine code — skipping interpretation entirely for those sections.

Result? Java gets the portability of an interpreter + the speed of a compiler. Best of both worlds.

🤔 𝗝𝗩𝗠 𝘃𝘀 𝗝𝗥𝗘 𝘃𝘀 𝗝𝗗𝗞 — 𝗜 𝘄𝗮𝘀 𝗰𝗼𝗻𝗳𝘂𝘀𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝘁𝗵𝗲𝘀𝗲 𝗳𝗼𝗿 𝗪𝗘𝗘𝗞𝗦:
𝗝𝗗𝗞 (𝗝𝗮𝘃𝗮 𝗗𝗲𝘃𝗲𝗹𝗼𝗽𝗺𝗲𝗻𝘁 𝗞𝗶𝘁) → For developers. Has the compiler, debugger, everything to write and run Java.

𝗝𝗥𝗘 (𝗝𝗮𝘃𝗮 𝗥𝘂𝗻𝘁𝗶𝗺𝗲 𝗘𝗻𝘃𝗶𝗿𝗼𝗻𝗺𝗲𝗻𝘁) → For end users. Has the JVM + libraries to run Java apps. No compiler.

𝗝𝗩𝗠 (𝗝𝗮𝘃𝗮 𝗩𝗶𝗿𝘁𝘂𝗮𝗹 𝗠𝗮𝗰𝗵𝗶𝗻𝗲) → The engine inside JRE that actually executes bytecode.
Think of it like this:
🏗️ JDK = Full workshop (build + run)
📦 JRE = Just the engine room (run only)
⚙️ JVM = The engine itself
